Mesoporous $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film (Figure 1d) was fabricated on the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ compact layer by spin-coating a paste containing $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ NPs, terpineol, ethylcellulose, and isopropyl alcohol, and was subjected to annealing at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The energy levels of the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film were studied by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and UV–vis spectroscopy, as shown in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. The VBM of $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ was determined to be $-5.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to vacuum level; this result is consistent with previous reports.[11,17] The conduction band minimum (CBM) of $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ was deduced to be $-1.68\mathrm{eV},$ which is above the CBM of the perovskite film. The compatible energy levels make it efficient to extract the photoinduced holes from the perovskite into the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ and to block electrons from injecting into the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ . The existence of the ultrathin $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ $(<10\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) underlying the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ mesoporous film, working as the compact layer, can avoid the direct contact between fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) and the perovskite in consideration of the mesoporous nature of the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film. Additionally, the graded energy alignment of the VBMs between $\mathrm{NiO_{x},}$ , $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ , and perovskite, as shown in Figure 2b, is beneficial for carrier transfer and collection, which is prerequisite to achieving excellent device performance.[18]
To testify the efficacy of such brand-new mesoporous inorganic-HTM-based PSCs, a control device with a planar structure, i.e., with planar $\mathsf{C u G a O}_{2}$ film as an HTM, was fabricated. Figure S5 in the Supporting Information shows the surface scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of perovs­ kite films formed on mesoporous (mp) $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ and on planar $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ . Both films show full surface coverage and dense film morphology with crystal grains in the range of $150{-}350~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating that the formation of mesopores within $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ does not significantly affect the crystallization of the perovskite film in comparison with the planar film. The root mean square (RMS) roughness of the perovskite films were both identified to be ${\approx}11$ nm by atomic force microscopy (AFM), as shown in Figure S6 in the Supporting Information. To achieve the ideal PSC performance, we have made two-step optimization of the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ -perovskite system: At first, the thickness of mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ was studied while the process parameters of perovskite film was fixed. It was found that the thickness of $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ films (ranging from 70 to $260~\mathrm{nm}$ ) posed an important influence on the thicknesses of perovskite films (as shown in Figure S7b–e in the Supporting Information). The comparison was presented in Figure $\mathtt{S7a}$ in the Supporting Information for the PCEs of the resulting PSCs with different thickness of mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ as HTM. A volcanic curve was formed. The average PCEs of the present PSCs varied from $17.5\%$ to $19.5\%$ and peaked for the device with an mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ thickness of ${\approx}130$ nm. We then systematically investigated the fabrication processing parameters for perovskite film, including spin speed, annealing temperature, the precursor concentration and so on. A typical crosssectional SEM image for the best mesoscopic device (shown in Figure 2a) shows that the thickness of each layer is 8, 130, 600, 60, and $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ for compact $\mathrm{NiO_{x},}$ mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ , perovskite, $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ and BCP, and $\operatorname{Ag}$ , respectively. To reveal the role of the present mesoporous HTM, a planar PSC with a planar $\mathsf{C u G a O}_{2}$ layer with similar thickness was also prepared for comparison. Figure 2c and Figure S8 in the Supporting Information present the $J{-}V$ characteristics of the best devices with planar and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ as HTMs under standard AM 1.5 illumination (at $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ . The main photovoltaic parameters, including short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ , open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ , fill factor (FF), and PCE, are summarized in Table  1. The control champion planar device exhibits a PCE of $17.37\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}={}$ $22.17~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $\ensuremath{V_{\mathrm{OC}}}=1080\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mV}},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=72.48\%$ , whereas the PCE for the champion device with mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ HTM is jumped to $20.13\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=22.23\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1132~\mathrm{mV},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=79.96\%$ . The improvement of the device efficiency is mainly attributed to the significantly enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF for the inverted mp- ${\cdot}\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ -based device.
As a control, PSC devices with only either $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ film or mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film as HTM were studied. The corresponding device parameters are provided in Figure S9 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information. PCEs of $15.93\%$ and $16.41\%$ were obtained for $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -only and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ -only cells, respectively. Both are significantly inferior to the device with the combination of the mesoporous $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}/\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{x}}$ HTMs. It is worth noting that the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the devices are lower than the previously reported results based on NiO and PTAA.[9a,19] The lower $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ for the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ -only device $(1051~\mathrm{mV})$ could be attributed to the ultrathin thickness of our $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ film (with thickness of only ${\approx}8~\mathrm{nm}^{\cdot}$ ) and the relatively lower crystallinity of $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ (due to the low temperature processing). The lower $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ for mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}.$ - only device $(1023~\mathrm{mV})$ should be ascribed to the absence of compact layer, which may induce partial contact of FTO and perovskite. To further confirm the importance of the hole compact layer, PTAA was used as the hole-transporting layer (HTL). As shown in Figure S9 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information, $1088\mathrm{mV}$ of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ was obtained, approaching the results reported by Zhu and co-workers $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}}V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1100~\mathrm{mV}$ for the device without secondary growth of the perovskite).[19c] When the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ were combinatively used as the bilayer HTL, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of our champion device is boosted to $1132\mathrm{mV},$ which is on par with the results based on PTAA reported by Huang and co-workers $'V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1140~\mathrm{mV})$ ).[3d] Therefore, the synergistic role of the compact $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ was critical in achieving efficient PSCs with high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ in this study.
In order to further broaden the application scope of $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}/m p$ $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ HTL, wide-bandgap perovskite with Eg of $1.73\ \mathrm{~eV}$ was employed as the light harvester. As shown in Figure S9 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information, $1181~\mathrm{mV}$ of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ can be obtained, which is higher than the results based on PTAA $\mathrm{'}V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1150~\mathrm{mV}$ for perovskite with $\mathrm{Eg}=1.72\ \mathrm{eV}_{.}$ ,[3d] and is close to the highest $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ reported for wide-bandgap PSCs ( $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is over $1220~\mathrm{mV},$ $\mathrm{Eg}=1.74~\mathrm{eV})$ .[3f] Nevertheless, there is still plenty of room to further push $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ up to an even higher level after systematical optimization of the inverted widebandgap PSCs based on $\Nu\mathrm{iO}_{\mathrm{x}}/m p\thinspace\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ .
Figure 2d shows the corresponding incident-photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra for the two cells with both $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ planar film and mesoporous film. The integrated photocurrent densities are 21.36 and $21.82~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for devices with planar and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ HTM, respectively, which are approximate to the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values extracted from the corresponding $J{-}V$ curves. Compared to the planar PSCs, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) value for the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ mesoscopic PSCs in the short wavelength range is almost the same while it is much higher in the long wavelength range beyond $550\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This pheno­menon should be ascribed to the pronounced light scattering in the mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film, which further increased the light absorption in perovskite layer (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Similar results were obtained from the optical transmission spectra. As shown in Figure S11 in the Supporting Information, mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ shows higher transparency than FTO and planar $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ in the range of $500{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , attributable to the light scattering and reduced reflection. The higher transparency of the $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ mesoscopic films will increase the light absorption of the perovskite and produce a higher photo­current. Specifically, higher $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ can be obtained in this case. Steady-state output efficiency measured at the maximum power point voltage $(V_{\mathrm{mpp}})$ was then studied to accurately assess the performance of the mesoscopic and the planar devices, as shown in Figure 2e and Figure S12 in the Supporting Information. The PCE of the mesoscopic device stabilizes at ${\approx}19.95\%$ , which is among the highest efficiency currently reported for inverted PSCs with inorganic HTMs.[12,19a,20] In contrast, the planar device presents a steady output efficiency of $16.72\%$ . These results demonstrate the superiority of the $\Nu\mathrm{iO_{x}/C u G a O_{2}}$ mesoporous structure in achieving high-efficiency PSC devices. To assess the reproducibility of our devices, 30 individual cells for the planar and mesoscopic devices were fabricated; the detailed parameters are shown in Figure 3. It is clear that all the key $J{-}V$ parameters of the mp ${\mathrm{CuGaO}}_{2}.$ - based PSCs are better, and the corresponding distributions are narrower in comparison with the planar devices. The results confirm the reliability of our method to fabricate high-efficiency inverted PSCs using graded $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}/$ mesoporous $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$
HTMs. Remarkably, it can also be seen that the improved performance is directly related to the enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF. The enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ should be related to the reduced interfacial defects.[18a] High FF $(>75\%)$ usually means the absence of large carrier extraction/injection barrier for the interface between perovskite and HTM/ETM.[21] The FF of the present mesoscopic devices is between $76\%$ and $80\%$ , indicating that the mesoscopic device structure and graded energy alignment can effectively improve carrier transfer and collection.
The stability of the device is a critical issue in determining the application potential for PSCs in the future. Therefore, the longterm stability of the PSC device was investigated. Figure 4a–d and Table S2 in the Supporting Information show the main parameters for the unencapsulated planar and mesoscopic devices. The PCE retains more than $90\%$ of its initial efficiency after being stored for two months under ambient conditions for the mesoscopic one. Moreover, less than $20\%$ of the efficiency loss can be observed even after $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ thermal aging at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (shown in Figure 4e), demonstrating its superior thermal stability and high tolerance to humidity. The excellent thermal stability of the present mesoscopic PSCs could be attributed to two factors: 1) the (FA,Cs) double cations perovskite adopted in this work is a thermally stable perovskite system in comparison with the methylammonium (MA)-containing perovskites.[22] 2) More importantly, the inorganic $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}/$ mesoporous $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ are even more thermally stable than the (FA,Cs) double cations perovskite.
To understand the large enhancement of the photovoltaic (PV) performance, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ as a function of light intensity (0.1 sun $\leq$ light intensity $\leq1$ sun) was measured and a linear correlation can be found, as shown in Figure  5a. The planar device gives a slope of 0.97, and the mesoscopic cell presents a slope of 0.98. Both data approach 1, indicative of no space-charge effect for both devices,[23] and confirming the negligible bimolecular recombination (a reverse process of light absorption) for both devices.[24] These results are in good agreement with recently reported results that the bimolecular charge carrier recombination in perovskite film is negligible at low light intensity, and it begins to dominate only when the light intensity is around 100 suns.[25] The predominant species in perovskites are free charge carrier rather than excitons due to that the exciton binding energy for perovskites at room temperature is quite low. Therefore, trap-assisted monomolecular recombination of electrons or holes is the main recombination process in a PSC.[26] We then studied the relationship between $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and light intensity (as shown in Figure 5b). The slope was decreased from $1.51\mathrm{kT}\mathrm{q}^{-1}$ for the planar cell to $1.38\mathrm{kT}\mathrm{q}^{-1}$ for the mesoscopic device, meaning that the trap-assisted recombination is significantly reduced due to the well-improved interface resulting from $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ mesoporous structure and the graded energy alignment.[27] The space-charge limited current (SCLC) method was further employed to examine the defect states in the perovskite layer. Figure S13 in the Supporting Information shows the corresponding current–voltage (I–V) curves measured from a hole-injecting device. Three distinct regions can be identified in the $I{-}V$ curve: an Ohmic region with the order $n=1$ , a Child region with $n=2$ , and a trap-filling limited (TFL) region with $n>3$ . The voltage marking the transition from the Ohmic region to the TFL region, $V_{\mathrm{TFL}},$ is used to calculate the trap-state densities close to the VBM.[28] According to Equation (1):
$$
V_{\mathrm{TFL}}=\frac{e n_{\mathrm{t}}L^{2}}{2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}}}
$$
where $e$ is the elementary charge, $n_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the trap density, $L$ is the thickness of the crystal between two Au electrodes, $\varepsilon$ is the relative dielectric constant of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ $(\varepsilon=46.9)$ ,[29] and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ is the dielectric constant of vacuum. The $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ of the devices based on planar and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ are 0.12 and $0.07~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. Therefore, the hole trap state density was reduced from $1.14\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for the pristine ones to $6.54\times10^{14}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for the mesoscopic device, which agrees well with the conclusion from Figure 5b that the mesoscopic device results in a reduced trap recombination. Additionally, from the Child region in Figure S13 in the Supporting Information, the carrier mobility can be extracted by the Mott–Gurney law.[30] The hole mobility was calculated to be $1.01\times10^{-2}$ and $2.33\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-\mathrm{i}}$ , respectively, for the planar and the mesoscopic structured perovskites, suggestive of the improved charge transfer. In short, the formation of the mesoscopic structure and graded energy alignment in the mesoporous- $\mathrm{.CuGaO}_{2}$ -based PSCs can reduce trap state density and suppress charge recombination, which is highly beneficial for obtaining excellent device performance.
The carrier kinetics of the planar and mesoscopic PSCs devices were then studied by the steady and time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopies. Figure 5c shows that the pristine perovskite exhibits a strong steady PL peak near the edge of the bandgap, while the introduction of $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ quenches the signal by $60\%$ . Noticeably, the combination of the compact $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ layer and the planar $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ quenches the PL signal by $94\%$ . This band edge PL signal was further quenched by over $96\%$ when the compact $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ and the mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ was combinatively used as bilayered HTM. These results demonstrate that the formation of mesoscopic structure with graded energy alignment plays a positive role in facilitating the charge transfer from perovskite to the HTMs with less energy loss; thus, superior hole-collection could be expected for the mesoscopic structured PSCs. Similar results were further provided by the time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectroscopy (shown in Figure 5d). The TRPL decay curves are fitted by the biexponential function (Equation (2)) and the fitted data are summarized in Table S3 in the Supporting Information.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{f\left(t\right)=A_{1}\exp^{\left(-t/\tau_{1}\right)}+A_{2}\exp^{\left(-t/\tau_{2}\right)}+B}\end{array}
$$
where $\tau_{1}$ is the fast transient component that corresponds to the surface property, and $\tau_{2}$ is the slow component that comes from the bulk property. $B$ is a constant for the baseline offset. $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ are the constants representing the contributions of the fast and slow components, respectively.[31] For the pristine perovskite, the PL decay times are $\tau_{1}=2.41$ ns and $\tau_{2}=432.9$ ns. For the sample with single $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ as HTM, the slow decay lifetime $(\tau_{2})$ was reduced to 298.7  ns. When the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ was combined with $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ as bilayer HTM, $\tau_{2}$ values were further decreased to 69.27 ns for the planar sample, and 23.62  ns for the mesoscopic one, respectively, indicating that the formation of the mesoscopic structure with graded energy alignment is more efficient in realizing carrier separation and transfer than the planar structure. The average PL decay lifetime can also be obtained via Equation (3):
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}=\frac{\sum A_{i}\tau_{i}^{2}}{\sum A_{i}\tau_{i}}
$$
The $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ for the pristine perovskite-only sample is $419.8~\mathrm{ns}$ , indicative of its high quality film.[2,3d,19b] The average PL decay time of the perovskite is reduced to 284.2 ns upon introducing the $\mathrm{NiO_{x}}$ compact layer. Moreover, PL decay times of the perovskite were further decreased to 49.97 and 16.56  ns, respectively, for the $\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{x/}}$ planar $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ film and $\Nu\mathrm{IO}_{\mathrm{x}}/m p\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ films, meaning that the holes transfer faster for the bilayered mesoscopic structure than the planar one. It is well known that the reduced PL decay time is beneficial for suppressing the charge recombination at the interface and for effectively improving device performance. We then performed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) to study the interfacial carrier transfer and transport kinetics. Figure S14 in the Supporting Information shows the Nyquist plots of the devices based on planar and mp $\mathrm{CuGaO}_{2}$ . The arcs at high frequency should be ascribed to carrier transport features in the bulk layer and the incomplete semicircles in the low frequency represent carrier recombination process at the interfaces.[27a,32] The corresponding values of $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ (internal series resistance), $R_{\mathrm{tr}}$ (transport resistance), and $R_{\mathrm{re}}$ (recombination resistance) were fitted by Z-view software with the given equivalent circuit and summarized in Table S4 in the Supporting Information. The mesoscopic device displays a lower $R_{\mathrm{tr}}$ and higher $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ compared to the planar one. This result indicates a faster carrier transfer and a lower recombination rate and is in agreement with the TRPL results. Therefore, the PV parameters for the mesoscopic-structured PSCs are significantly enhanced in comparison with the planar PSCs. The present brand-new structure can not only increase the contact area of perovskite/HTM and shorten the hole diffusion pathways in the perovskite layer, which favors the charge separation and interfacial trap state passivation. More importantly, the bilayered HTM structure results in the formation of graded energy alignment at the interface, leading to favorable band bending at the valence band edge for improved carrier transporting between each layer.[18b] All of these characteristics can effectively enhance carrier transfer and depress carrier recombination. As a result, high-efficiency PSCs can be obtained for the mesoscopic-structured PSCs in this study.
In summary, we have developed a strategy to improve both the efficiency and stability of inverted PSCs by designing a bilayer inorganic mesoporous HTM. The formation of mesoporous structure and well matched energy levels of the VBMs improve the charge carrier transfer and collection, reduce interfacial trap states. Additionally, the device made of the mesoscopic structure has achieved a stabilized PCE of $\approx20\%$ , which is superior to that of the planar cell $(16.72\%)$ . Moreover, the inorganic-HTM-based device exhibits excellent long-term thermal stability. Our findings indicate that the rational design of a suitable device configuration may be an effective method for attaining efficient and stable PSCs.
## Experimental Section
Device Fabrication: Devices were prepared on cleaned and patterned FTO substrates. The dense $\mathsf{N i O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ was prepared via electron beam evaporation. And then $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of ${\mathsf{C u C a O}}_{2}$ powdered materials were mixed with $400~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ isopropyl alcohol. This mixture was then ultrasonicated for $30\mathrm{\min}$ and poured dropwise in $3.32~\mathsf{m L}$ of Terpineol and $4m L$ of isopropyl alcohol/ethylcellulose (9:1) to form the paste with a suitable viscosity, and then the paste was deposited on $\mathsf{N i O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ -coated FTO substrates (Pilkington TEC8, ${\mathsf{14}}{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}{\mathsf{s q}}^{-1}.$ ) by spin coating. This mp $\mathsf{C u C a O}_{2}$ films can be obtained after annealing at $350~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30{\mathsf{m i n}}$ to burn off the organic. For comparison, the planar ${\mathsf{C u C a O}}_{2}$ film was prepared by directly spin coating the isopropyl alcohol suspension. The perovskite layer was deposited by using a green solvent–processed method in a nitrogen-filled glovebox. The precursor solution was comprised of $52~\mathrm{mg}$ of CsI, $\boldsymbol{174}\:\mathrm{mg}$ of FAI, $560~\mathsf{m g}$ of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{r}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{{17}}\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{mg}}}$ of FABr in $1m L$ of dimethyl formamide (DMF) and dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) (4:1, v/v). The solution was spin-coated onto the ${\mathsf{C u C a O}}_{2}$ substrates by a consecutive two-step spin-coating process at 1000 and 6000  rpm for 10 and $40\mathrm{~s~}$ , respectively. During the second spin-coating step, $120~\upmu\upiota$ Anisole was dropped onto the substrate after $20\thinspace s$ . And then the substrate was annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . An electron-transporting layer was coated with a solution of $\mathsf{P C}_{67}\mathsf{B M}$ dissolved in anisole $(20\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ at 3000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and followed by a saturated BCP solution at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}.$ . Finally, a $\mathsf{l o o}\mathsf{n m}$ thick Ag electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation to complete the device fabrication.
Characterization: The XRD was measured on Maxima 7000 diffractometer (Shimadzu, Japan) with a Cu Ka radiation (40  kV, $\mathsf{100~m A}_{\iota}$ . Energy levels of ${\mathsf{C u C a O}}_{2}$ were detected by Thermo escalab $250\times1$ . The film morphology was obtained by using a Titachi S5200 field-emission SEM (Hitachi High Technologies Corporation). The UV– vis spectra were measured using an Evolution 201 spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific Corporation). The NP morphology, elemental mapping, and lattice spacing were examined by TEM (Carl Zeiss SMT Pte, Ltd Libra 200FE) at $200\ \mathsf{k V}.$ The steady PL spectra and time-resolved PL decay measurements were performed using an HORIBA DeltaFlex system (HORIBA) with an excitation wavelength at $510~\mathsf{n m}$ . Repetition rates were 100 and $2M H z$ , respectively. The AFM data were obtained on a Dimension Icon (broker Corporation). Calibrating of roughness value, RMS, and graphics processing were taken on NanoScope Analysis. The current–voltage characteristics were measured by Keithley 2400 source and the solar simulator with standard AM 1.5G ( $\mathsf{l o o}\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , SSF5-3A:
Enlitech) under ambient conditions. The $J{-}V$ curves were measured by forward $(-0.7$ to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.2V}}$ forward bias) or reverse (1.2 to −0.1 V) scans. $J-V$ curves for all devices were obtained by masking the cells with a metal mask $0.09\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}$ in area. Monochromatic IPCE spectra were recorded as functions of wavelength with a monochromatic incident light of $1\times10^{16}$ photons $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ in alternating current (AC) mode with a bias voltage of 0 V (QE-R3011). The light intensity of the solar simulator was calibrated by a standard silicon solar cell provided by PV Measurements.
